Chapter 3 - The Love of Self
When I first came across the philosophical mandate ‘Know thyself’, I mistook it for an informational request: to fill in some sort of unknown with data about oneself. I have since realized that the wisdom of self-knowledge does not sit in procedurally fulfilling the request, but in honoring its implications for oneself. Collectively, we have achieved a monumental level of self-knowledge: we understand the human species better than any other. We know our biology, our behavioral patterns, our hard-wired evolutionary instincts, our limitations and weaknesses with precision. But we haven’t used this knowledge with love.
Take, for instance, our biological preference for sweet and salty foods—it’s a hard-wired trait of humans to seek out energetically rich and scarce resources. We fulfilled the mandate; we “know” this about our biology. Yet, we used this knowledge to manufacture destruction. We mass-produced artificially sweetened “food” that is cheaper and more accessible than anything grown in soil. We weaponized our biology against us, creating a global health crisis that forced the World Health Organization to intervene. We knew our nature, but we weaponized it for profit. The market for ultra-processed foods is around $5 trillion. In contrast, organic food and drink only reached approximately €145 billion. In this market, the passion people have for food science is recruited into work that achieves the opposite of what science intended to do for humanity. The bliss point, which describes the exact combination of salt and sugar that turns any food into something our taste buds will crave on first bite, is our evidence that ‘knowing ourselves’ is not an information mandate, but an ethical one.
Something similar is happening with the love of self. By the time you read this chapter, you will have already bought into artificially gratifying products, lifestyles, and beliefs designed to elicit feelings that emulate self-love but never provide it. We have figured similar ‘bliss points’ in our relationship with ourselves and commercialized them. Behind-the-scenes, the algorithms that never sleep have tracked your vulnerabilities, sensitivity to emotionally charged content, and framed products as answers to your ‘self-love’ cravings.
You have been offered a menu surface-level fixes for deep-seated needs; all in the name of fabricated demand. Shaped by marketing narratives, self-evolved has devolved into little more than a programmatic lie. This lie is fabricated by individuals whose income relies on convincing you that you don’t love yourself.
I can attest to this, having studied and practiced online marketing since 2008. During that time, no psychology trick was left unexploited in the relentless pursuit of capturing your attention, interest, and ultimately, your money.
Self-love has turned into a programmatic lie. It’s more often than not fabricated by people whose earnings depend on making you believe you don’t love yourself. I know because I have studied and practiced online marketing since 2008. I left the industry as soon as programmatic advertising became sophisticated enough to use ‘dark patterns’ rather than explicit interest to exploit the subconscious workings of desire and identity. Before I left the ‘demand marketing’ world, any skilled programmatic marketer could optimize ads to trigger impulse buying. Today, algorithms actively exploit emotions. A World Economic Forum report suggests that over 70% of ad impressions are delivered based on emotional states algorithmically inferred through eye-tracking, scroll speed and attention markers. The algorithmic machine does not stop there: most social media is filled with contagious, addictive content exploiting cognitive biases and under-regulated emotions. The self-love industry does not care about you; it cares that you believe it. To a dysregulated woman, anyone can prescribe a dosage of ‘100 repetitions of powerful self-love affirmations’ for no other reason than to drive viewership. Anyone with LLM(large language model) prompting and agentic AI skills can write self-love books that claim to rewire your brain. This is a programmable sort of self-love––the kind we believe we can install and upgrade like apps on our phone.
There was a time, though, when the love of self was an unaltered happening––like a smile in your cheeks and a buoyant feeling expanding in your chest. It was like twirling in a dreamy pink princess dress, utterly unbothered. Your movements were instinctive, sensual, and filled with pure joy—a dance that spoke of deep self-adoration, needing no instruction. Or perhaps it was the thrill of diving into painting, writing, coding, or building, lost in the flow until you finally gazed upon your finished creation, witnessing the purest act of self-love blossom into your most magnificent masterpiece.
Such is the unaltered spirit of the love of self. Yet, somewhere along the way it we lost touch with it and found fickle comfort in its substitute.
Programmable self-love
My first contact with programmable self-love came through therapy––a trusted, safe channel meant to allow me to explore my psyche without judgement. My coaches used traditional and non-traditional therapeutic methods for analysis, exploration, and healing. One day, it was suggested to me that I do not love myself enough. 
My mind reeled with disbelief at the diagnosis. ‘What do you mean? I’ve fought for my advancement in my career and life, stood up for myself, invested in myself.’ If this isn’t self-love, I must have missed something. I was convinced that the root of my problems stemmed from systemic injustice, bias against women, the weariness of daily life, and major life challenges, rather than from a lack of self-love. As far as I was concerned, I had plenty of self-love to last a lifetime, and yet the lie still got to me. Within a week, the content I consumed shapeshifted before my eyes: ‘Here is how you know you don’t love yourself’, ‘If you love yourself, you do these three things’. They all sounded reasonable and relatable, and they seemed to follow my mood like a shadow: when my eyes lingered a little longer on a ‘self-love’ teaching, I received more recommendations to attend healing classes. When I discarded these recommendations, my content feed offered alternatives: maybe a lipstick? How about a getaway?
You see, I have always been a curious learner who didn’t mind flexing viewpoints. I was open to being proven that self-love was the answer to my woes. I was open to reflecting on the possibility that my self-love might have been tampered with. Shortly after, I was persuaded. Not for lack of judgement, but because I was desperate for a solution to my misery. For many years until this point, I had clung onto ‘rational’ ways of dealing with adversity in life, and those ways stopped working altogether. 
Before I sought relief in self-love, there was a time when I was overly rational––and I must confess––I suffered from the insufferable intellectual woman syndrome. I gobbled up nonfiction literature on how one should self-optimize and be in the world; most of it was written by white, privileged men, but I read it with excessive interest, almost as if I too aspired to be one. I was oblivious to the works of many brilliant people––women and people sidelined as minorities––who did not get to be in the spotlight. As an insufferable intellectual woman, I would quote from books like “The God Delusion” to protect my atheistic beliefs, and from determinist-mechanistic, behaviorist-reductionist literature to dismiss the role of emotions. 
Until life happened, with its unending setbacks, disappointments, and disillusionments, and my intellectual throne stood useless on a deserted landscape scorched by rationalistic brilliance. I had to get off the throne to find the lush wetlands of my soul. So, I embraced what is often classified as ‘mystical’ and ‘alternative’. From being a hardcore rational atheist, I grew more acquainted with spirituality. 
When I fell off the pedestal of intellectualism, I gave the ‘opposing’ approach a chance. This isn’t new. Throughout their lives, people swing from one extreme (rationalism) to the other (mysticism) because their psyche is starved and is craving wholeness. That’s why we see ex-CEOs turn yoga instructors, ex-scientists turn artists, and boss girls finally embracing slow living. This U-turn, from one behavior to its opposite, is the psyche’s way of compensating for what it had missed out on for too long.
When I was ‘diagnosed’ with a lack of self-love, I was ready for my U-turn from intellectualism. I agreed to the diagnosis because I longed for a cure; a dash of hope in a string of unfortunate events. And then, something happened. As soon as I accepted the premise that I “lacked self-love’, I performed the symptoms and behaved accordingly. 
What do people with low self-love behave like you may ask? This may sound familiar. I shielded myself from praise and support to prove I’m self-sufficient… I rejected affection because I could love myself even better than anyone could… I only accepted love and generosity when it was obvious I deserved it. I agreed to others’ ‘self-love’ boundaries even when I saw our friendship starve and collapse.
Self-love advocates behave like this because they believe we need to love ourselves more (and need others less) or that we have to put ourselves first (and avoid depending on others). So, I too behaved a bit like that, like the woman who was prescribed to love herself more. I masked my normal desire for connection to ensure my self-love is (strong) enough. I nourished myself and met my needs, but still felt famished after each self-pampering ritual. And felt even more famished in relationships. 
In a purely iatrogenic way, I developed additional symptoms: an unbothered facade that masked deteriorating self-esteem and performative positivity.
The iatrogenic effect of an ‘accepted diagnosis’ is well documented. It is what happens when you’re misdiagnosed with anxiety instead of a heart condition and you get medicated for nerves when you should care for your arteries. Women are 30-40% to be misdiagnosed this way because they are… well, the ‘emotional’ ones. The iatrogenic harm that results from misdiagnosis and believing your diagnosis is well documented in healthcare, but not so much in the self-love arena.
When every interaction is filtered through the lens of “How does this serve my healing and highest good?” or “Is this person honoring my boundaries?”, the prescription to prioritize self-love can lead to unintended consequences or ‘iatrogenic effects’. These might include pushing people away or becoming a bit more difficult to be around. To “love myself,” I disconnected from the empathy essential for deep connection and became less tolerant of the natural friction that strengthens authentic relationships. The industry thrives on the tendency to adopt and act on such diagnoses. To sell a cure, the industry must first sell you a sickness. The cure creates more dysfunction, like narcissistic behavior. It’s iatrogenic. 
The phenomenon of adopting labels based on one’s diagnosis, known as the ‘looping effect,’ was first discussed by Ian Hawkings. In the realm of self-love, human labels like ‘the dark feminine,’ ‘clean girl,’ or ‘boss girl’ emerge from the collective consciousness as tropes. These tropes then feed new narratives into the cultural branding and advertising industries. Laila van Berg, a published modern philosopher, examines dark feminine tropes through a socio-economic lens. She argues that these tropes not only warp behaviors but also trap women in middle-class inequalities. To embody a femme fatale, one must frequent specific places, adopt a certain appearance, and associate with particular people—all of which require capital and consumption. Consequently, most ‘feminine tropes’ are nothing but aspirational fairytales for the middle class. They benefit the market more than they foster genuine self-love. 
My wake-up call came after I tried to maximize and produce self-love through regimented rituals. They don’t always look regimented, no, but some of them are. “Repeat these affirmations 30 days in a row and your world will change”. “Get my $600 course with one 1:1 coaching hour and reclaim your power back”. Inner child work: $1200. Self-love books: $300. It continued in cycles. I’d pause, hit a wall, and start again. Self-love to self-loathing on repeat. Sometimes I got myself into debt, repaid it, then went right back into it because––as some of you might have guessed it––you can’t love yourself in 30 days. Self-love is not programmable. 
The first time self-love was introduced as a ‘programmable’ concept was in the early 2000, after the practice of Neuro-Linguistic Programming (NLP) had already established itself in the market and in the collective mentality. NLP was invented by a mathematician and psychology professor; but its roots–allegedly in maths and computer science–easily disclose a dangerous assumption: that the mind can be programmed like you program software. Repeat your affirmations for 21 days in a row and your newly installed self-love program will show results. That is not true, but it is credible. If you accept that the mind is like software, you become a follower. As a follower, you will buy NLP to reprogram your mind. 
The irony is truly painful because the argument is circular and self-defeating. First, it is stated that, from childhood to adulthood, you’re programmed to believe certain things that impede self-love. This ‘programming’ is not a revolutionary concept; it is called socialization and introjection, but in NLP it receives a sophisticated and convincing label, ‘programmed beliefs’, so that the entire story holds. Then, according to NLP, in order to fix your negative programs, you must clear them, like you clear your cache, and re-program more desirable beliefs in place. It’s a never-ending loop of deleting, uninstalling thoughts like software bugs and then re-installing, re-programming new thoughts like shiny new apps. In fact, some metaphors used in NLP are literal parallels to the software world. The circularity of this paradigm is nauseating and self-defeating, but credible. Unsurprisingly, it comes from a male-dominated school of thought, where intuition, feelings, and the unconscious are labeled as illogical and dangerous. Something to tame and control. And so, NLP evolved into a 50-year long practice that applied itself to any human aspiration: to be more productive, more resilient, more likable, more loved. Use this self-love program, it’s $50 and will change your life. 
When self-love methods lost their effectiveness in helping me cope with personal and inter-relational problems, I turned to another, and another. I read books on self-healing topics and self-assessed my wounds to death. I got this eerie feeling that I had to postpone any social involvement until I was healed enough. It turns out I wasn’t the only one suffering from induced feelings of inadequacy whilst trying to love and heal myself.
Some of my friends chimed in, “I don’t think you should date until you fix your issues”. It was tempting to agree, as we were all caught up in this self-healing obsession, but deep inside the remark didn’t sit right: which issues? They keep piling up. When will I be healed enough? 
Then my memory came back. As a developing child and young adult, I remembered it was perfectly fine for me to live life whichever way. Upset or joyful, ill or healthy, imperfect or just as I was. There was no assumption that only children who ‘are developed enough, healthy enough, or good enough could enter family dynamics’. People become parents even if they’re not 100% ready to be parents. Many of us became lovers, friends, founders, and whatnot with no need to be prepared for the role. There’s no graduation ritual, although there are many transition rituals we observe in various cultures to mark the change from ‘single’ to ‘married’, from ‘child’ to adult. However, self-healing as a pre-requisite self-love was uprooted from mental health and transplanted into an industry that took human life and made it performative endeavour. 
The danger of programmable self-love
‘I am at peace, I am enough just as I am. I am at peace, I am enough.’ After ten repetitions, my breath felt forced, like I was lifting weights at the gym. I did not realize how much effort I was putting into these affirmations, until I felt my body tense up after the ninth repetition. At the gym, I push through as many reps, despite and in spite of the pain, I clench my jaws, until the pain shatters my resistance and it’s physically impossible to do my last rep. With the same grit, I powered through one or two more rounds of these affirmations. ‘Why does it have to be so hard? And why am I not seeing results?’––I asked.
It was the 5th month in a row where programming myself made no difference. My body was in constant shutdown. I could barely do chores around the house. Every time I washed dishes, I had to take a break after the 2nd dish washed. I powered through a 5km walk in my neighbourhood, on a lovely trail surrounded by trees. Soon, the walk itself felt so gloomy and repetitive, I called it my ‘death row walk’. Programming myself out of psychological collapse nearly broke my spirit.
Unaddressed pain and anger in the mind, and body do very nasty things to oneself. If becomes worse than depression––it’s as debilitating as having a spinal injury. Having tried so many traditional and untraditional healing methods, I also developed helplessness: I would no longer try anything, and if I did–it would be so robotic and forced, it felt like work. 
What happened next wasn’t a straightforward process. I first allowed myself fight back against anyone who told me I didn’t love myself enough. That simply was not the truth. The truth was that I had developed complex trauma because of life challenges, job loss, fear of losing my home, and after having lost too many friends and relationships, all in a few years. So, the process started with truth. 
Then, after a while, I came across cathartic practices that involved dancing and moving chaotically to express whatever the body needed to. Soon into this practice, I found myself punching the air, in an attempt to fight back and hurt those and that which hurt me. Then, I started screaming as well. A few sessions later after punching and screaming, I saw myself laughing and gesturing as if cutting bad things out of my life, pushing them away. Then I just danced. 
The big revelation came when I screamed. I must have startled all my neighbours. It didn’t matter. What happened next was that I realized loving myself meant being unlovable, too: pushing back, fighting back, punching back, screaming, swearing. It meant accepting that the process involved self-defeat and despondency. Anger and whatnot. Thankfully, the expression of unlovable behaviors and thoughts came out in a safe environment––for everyone’s safety. 
Nowadays, if I feel like I’m repressing a bit too much or try too hard to over-write the truth of what I am in the moment, I go back to my little practice. I yell it all out; I feel it, witness it, then go on with my day. 
At the other end of NLP and relentless self-healing, there’s the acceptance of one’s inner life with its suffering: the anxiety, the disappointment, the heartbreak. Instead of programming your unconscious and your ego, you make friends with both. As for suffering, you don’t fight it, you let it shape you. This is not an encouragement of martyrdom, no. Some people are more inclined to nurturing and holding others’ suffering. Therefore, the statement that suffering is part of life is dangerous for them, as they receive it as a call to self-sacrifice. The call to accept suffering to be more suitable to those who chase comfort and personal interests. So instead of prescribing NLP for self-love, I would prescribe caring for others. To avoid suffering, fight it, flee it–––that’s not strength, that is weakness. To face one’s and another’s suffering forges a greater capacity for love and compassion that programming ever would. 
No mud, no lotus - Thich Nhat Hanh
Moving on from self-love programs, another form of fabricated self-love is the kind that requires you to prove it, rather than program it. It is using a subtle technique. First, the brand or person finds a value statement you agree with–such as “I don’t have to prove myself” or “I’m focusing on myself” and repackages it as a product. Since you cannot contradict yourself, you find it nearly impossible to buy anything else but the products that sell you agreement with your values. 
The product can look like a physical object––a gym outfit, skincare; like a lifestyle that must be seen––doing yoga, traveling, going on solo dates; like a set of rules and behaviors that grant you access to friendships or groups––a VIP membership, ‘self-care girlies’; or a digital product and service.
To prove your self-love you have to perform it. You drink matcha lattes and photograph your ‘wellness choice’ as a virtue. You go on solo date nights to perform self-love and self-sufficiency. Personal branding and self-love content–-that’s streamed narcissism. And the self-care spas? Those are signalling status and sophistication rather than caring for your nervous system.
The love of self that you don’t need to prove looks like eating to nourish your body, befriending solitude, staying grounded in self-esteem, and taking breaks that work for you. Buying into self-love that requires proof is not sustainable nor wholesome. 
The Clean Girl aesthetic, for example, is one of the many soul-crushing trends that capitalize on women’s desire to be loved as they are. The Clean Girl trend was popularized by stunning models who look stunning even after a hangover. The women amongst us who need to do night shifts or put in 12 hours of work a day––well, we don’t always beam with beauty. At first sight, the Clean Girl aesthetic reclaims natural beauty, in contrast with the heavily-contoured, caked and glammed up aesthetic. The problem with the clean girl aesthetic is that is does something worse: it hides the hard labour of looking natural behind a hard-earned dewy look. The truth is, you need much more capital to afford the clean girl aesthetic. Even the simplest rituals, like using a gua-sha, imply that you can buy jade gua-shas and face rollers, know how to massage your face or have others do it for you and that you have time for skin care, more time than it takes to apply makeup. It takes more capital and more effort to be a clean girl. But, you cannot speak about it–you have to keep your work secret, otherwise it defeats the fantasy.
The commodification of wellness and love leaves you more hollow and sometimes less healed. Instead of regulating your nervous system, you perform wellness from a flight or flight state over which bath bombs are the fizziest, which oils activates your energy, setting your candles and making sure you glass of wine doesn’t spill over in your bath. The hidden labour of self-love defeats its purpose. Labouring for self-love is not quite consistent with itself. The answer is simply ‘no’ to working on your perfect spa-at-home fantasy, ‘Yes’ to working on your self.
Labouring for self-love comes with implicit expectations––that any investment into oils and potions will yield results; and those results must be speedily incorporated in one’s identity rebrand. Recent consumer research papers, from 2022 onwards, such as ‘The Commodity Society: Understanding the Self as a Product in a Market-Driven World’ by Ramy Ayoub shine a light on the destructive effects of commodified wellness and identities. Self-love becomes a side gig, an investment that must pay off, a topic of negotiation, a proof of status. With a self-love side gig, you work for the payoff more than you work on yourself. 
This book, however, is not meant to criticise the realities of our world, it’s meant to remind you of simple truths: you cannot buy, program, and prove what is already yours. You cannot program self-love in 30 days, nor summon it half-way into your perimenopause through kundalini. You cannot program it in 2 weeks, after you’ve broken up with your boyfriend either.
On the other hand, I am not ashamed of my self-love paraphernalia. You shouldn’t be either. Whether it’s one too many spa days that increased your credit balance more than your aura, personal coaching or even esoteric practices, there’s nothing to be ashamed of, anyway. It’s okay.
Let me tell you a secret. Even after I finished my training in GRC for AI, diving into scientific, papers and establishing a nae for myself, I felt no shame pulling out a tarot spread for myself. Let me tell you, there’s nothing to be ashamed of in engaging with esoteric, irrational practices. Whether you use the Roscharch test, body and energy readings, tarot, or art, they are all rooted in truth: the truth that a side of us is not programmable, it is illolgical, non-verbal, and must be conversed with as such. 
The market of spiritual healing has often promised self-love at the end of 40 minute crystal energy infused meditation session. Whilst services of this sort prove to be useless after a while, they tap into human experiences that are poweful, real and often beyond comprehension. 
Some approaches, like art therapy, give the psyche a free channel of symbolic expression, which helps process emotions and inner content better than any formal, linear logic ever could. Tarot cards and any divination that uses symbols give your unconscious, symbolic life a voice so you can feel deeply and find relief. And that’s perfectly okay; but they must be yours, sacred to you, unadultered by external prescriotions, nor gurus. 
‘You’re a guru’ –– Ram Das.
How I made friends with the Death card
The most feared card in the tarot deck is the death card. And I pulled it nearly every day, when my most painful relationship ended. I had developed such deep wounds from this connection, I looked to the esoteric to help me. The pain of unrequited love is harrowing, but it did something in me: it broke the veil between what is called my ego and my unconscious. From spiritual healing to astrology, dreamwork, and depth analysis, I plunged into practices that temporarily provided a bigger container for my emotions, meaning, and self-study than normal life with its narrow explanations and routines ever could. Many people, on the brink of an existential crisis and relentless emotional pain, succumb to psychosis and rarely return: they stay stuck in the expressions of woundedness that were meant to find cathartic relief, but become life long regressions. Others instinctively sense their breaking point approaching and venture into long, deliberate stretches of solitude, during which they seek to find their core. Some retreat from daily modern demands of the worl backpacking into th wilderness, others retreat into art, community work, meditation or simply solace. 
Sensing that the pull towards the esoteric was my retreat into the inner wilderness, I went fully in. I knew I had reached a point where I had to let my unconscious speak. My unconscious needed to shatter the ego defenses I had built to contain and repress it. It wanted me to finally go through my greatest fear and torment––loss, abandonment and immense grief––survive it all, and emerge on the other side. Knowing this, I allowed myself to get swept into magic and divination. 
This experience gave me the experiential understanding of concepts I had until then only became acquainted with theoretically. I understand what unconscious meant, because I could witness its expression, I noticed my projections, and experienced selflessness and detachment, I discovered bliss and excruciating pain, paralysing fear, dissociation, i understood what psychological contents are and why attaching oneself to those contents is a waste of inner life. 
But it’s all gave more the lived experience of what philosophers, mystics and academics had captured in words. This applies to self-love too. 
It was during my darkest time dealing with unrequited love that I created within me the stock of insight I would later use to rediscover the love of self authentically, 
 
When I first pulled the Death card, my heart sank. I was not ready to accept that I had to end the entire connection cold-turkey. I had powered through ‘no-contact’ for months without getting rid of the angst, the pain, the craving. The next day, the Death out came out again. Maybe I hadn’t shuffled enough? Maybe it’s a ‘transformation’ not an ending? And so, after many days of attempting to negotiate with my tarot spreads, I started paying attention to my own reactions. It was not the card, it was my reaction to the card that mattered. It gave me a clue where I was in my grief and addiction recovery from that man. Mind you, this was the second year I had tried to break this trauma bond. No therapy, hobby, work, or medicine helped. When your psyche ruptures after years of unkind interactions, when you’re addicted to hope rather than reality, traditional mental health simply does not work. And I had done everything: I was the successful woman with a career in tech, I was the former atheist-now-turned spiritual, I was the independent professional who had her life together. My rational efforts fell flat, whilst my unconscious railed with terror and anger.
So, I decided to dive fully into all irrational things, because clearly that’s what needed to happen. And tarot was one of those irrational things, where I allowed my unspoken, unfiltered projections to unfold, whilst I was making mental notes of them. And soon enough, the morning came when I pulled the Death card and I shrugged: “Okay, this is the end. So it is” And from then onwards, I made friends with my unconscious parts, I allowed them to speak to me in whichever way they wanted to, and I finally accepted and loved myself through the most devastating part of my life. 
To this truth, C.G. Jung speaks about symptoms and psychosis not as pathological; instead, irrational behaviors are the psyche’s best attempt to move forward with what it has available. 
In a way, even if we give into self-love tropes and illusions, perhaps it is a symptom of something else–but we must always find our way back to the knowing that we always have access to endless love towards ourselves. The rational woman knows this truth. She is fully open to her own nature, understands her nature and does not change it. 
The rational woman is unprogrammable, and so is her self-love. She won’t add bubble baths and spa days to her self-calendar, she will rest. She won’t need dark feminine dresses, her presence is enough. She can slip into the hardworking, focused adult or into the soft, sensual side without shame or props. Her divine feminine is not programmed with affirmations, nor debugged by kundalini yoga poses. She is unprogrammable because no script ignites who she truly is. Her self-love is unprogrammable because it is never scripted and acted, it simply acts. 
There are a few books that speak to the Rational woman’s deeper parts of the psyche. The Vital Spark: Reclaim Your Outlaw Energies and Find Your Feminine Fire by Lisa Marciano, Untamed by Glennon Doyle, Rage Becomes Her: The Power of Women’s Anger” by Soraya Chemal, Loud: Accept Nothing Less Than the Life You Deserve.
What is not fabricated is the love of self, which you were born with and will last you a lifetime. The love of self generates endlessly, it does not consume. It generates power, joy, resourcefulness out of thin air, out of nothing, like a miracle. Except, there’s nothing miraculous about the love of self–it is yours for life, from the moment you were born. And, most importantly, the love of self is unprogrammable.
No decade goes by without a brave, articulate, educated woman writes about the power of being themselves, particularly when that looks like being unpolished, loud, inconvenient to those who expect women to repress their inner world. 
And, we need to take this with a grain of salt. The level of unpallability that works for you, that gives your shadow energy and your self power, depends on your psyche. Some women operate between extremes, others within narrower ranges of experiences. You do not have to be obnoxiously unlovable to show your shadow that you can love and accept yourself. you simply need to be you. 
Fighting back against programmable self-love
When my self-love paraphernalia got too expensive to maintain and its results came short of promises, I woke up. I allowed myself to doubt there was a problem with me to begin with and went on a journey of honesty. Indeed, after all of this ‘self-work’ I had become aware of my shortcomings, including my tendency to be intellectual. 
However, I also realized that every human trait can be experienced in a productive or unproductive way. So, yes, intellectual as I am, what gift might come out of it? Could it be insight? Discernment? Fair game!
The next two months I spent analyzing five hundred reviews left by people about therapy and self-help tools available on the market. I gave my intellectual needs something to feast on, but the search was personal. Was I the only one caught in a self-help whirlwind and got spit out on the sidewalk because I could no longer stomach this self-love diet? It turns out, I was not alone. From the 600 reviews, a pattern emerged––one that looked just like mine, yet people were reporting from its different locations. 
In the beginning, you want to seek a diagnosis. x people appreciate the convenience self-assessment tools. Then, you want to be validated and heard. 131 people out of 600 gave rated highly platforms with a diverse range of therapies and approaches. People branched out from CBT and neurodiversity-informed help to dreamwork and emotional freedom tapping. I was one of them. I entered the maze of healing, sometimes adamant about such and such approach, refusing to work with people who did not share my frameworks. When options apear, people either eplore multiple at once or double down on a favorite with fanatical enthusiasm. 
Then something interesting happened. A portion of people stood out through small but deeply wise comments. They would say ‘no matter how many tools a platform has–reminders, journals, to-do lists––it as only when I did the work that I got better’. There’s something raw and true about this statement, and it will only make sense when you have done the work. 
As a gift to myself before my 40th birthday, I used some savings for a solo trip to Chichen Itza, Mexico – my first in over six years. My intention was to turn inward, heal, and complete the first chapter of this book. However, I arrived utterly exhausted and burnt out, still grappling with the grief of a devastating breakup. I found myself unable to do anything, often just sitting, too drained to even appreciate the Tulum beaches at my resort. I nearly cancelled my Chichen Itza tour, and when the day of the trip came, I reluctantly got into the van, expecting very little and yet secretly wanting that my contact with ancient places would somehow shake me out of despondency and bring me back to life.
Before reaching the Chichen Itza pyramid, I was dropped off about a mile away. Walking towards it along a straight, ancient path, built by indigenous tribes millennia ago, I encountered countless local vendors lining both sides. They offered handmade masks, souvenirs, trinkets, semi-precious stones, t-shirts, and a variety of other merchandise, catering to the hundreds of daily visitors. I was tempted to browse every stall. Despite the distractions, I resolved to walk directly to the pyramid, reminding myself of my purpose. This is not uncommon; sacred sites worldwide are often surrounded by distractions, offering fleeting joy, novelty, and sometimes promises of luck or healing.
The same applies to our inner selves. Many of us spend years outside our “inner temple,” captivated by a marketplace of self-love trinkets. Once we finally enter, we may find ourselves too tired, having already satisfied some desires, and forget why we came. We run out of time, eager to return to our routines, our work, our next meal. We forget the sacredness of our inner sanctuary. Upon returning home, with a suitcase full of souvenirs, the experience of entering such a sacred space often becomes a mere checklist item or a fading memory. 
That's a suitcase of paraphernalia we carry with ourselves. Each of us has one.
Have you opened your paraphernalia box of self-love tactics (I call it my Pandora’s box) only to discover that some give you a bit of sass today, and others drain you? Have you reflected on what that says about who you really are? My paraphernalia of skincare and makeup made me glow up but also reflected at me how much I want to glow and reach for that star aura. Have you tried all sorts of practices that didn’t work only to discover that they didn’t work because you weren’t honest with yourself? Then, have you tried sitting yourself down and doing the honest work of looking at who you really are, what you can and cannot do, what works for you and what doesn’t? And it it does not, have you done the honest work to accept that it is okay––you can let that forever wounded feminine become the female warrior? 
‘I have been called by the people who know me well a “wounded healer”. In fact I believe that in order to be an effective healer you have to have been broken down to understand what pain really is. So blessed are those caregivers who are able to use their pain as their greatest teacher.” - Naomi Judd
The honest, hard work is a battle that leads you back to yourself. A battle against many distractions, labels, prescriptions, and interventions to make the ‘self’ more palatable, lovable or hip. A battle against cultural tides and commercial vacuums, against dark patterns and algorithms profiling your vulnerability just to sell you a cure at the right moment. Ads injected in your chatGPT conversations when you’re confessing your shame, your worries, your desperation. This warriorship goes against everything that self-love teaches: it is not the self-care ritual you should fight for, it is a fairer lifestyle for everyone where self-care is a natural part of the day, not a posh ritual; it is not the glam aesthetic you should rebel against, it is the subversive industry of ‘effortless beauty’––because the capital and labour required for such effortless beauty is vastly for more substantail that going full glam; it is not self-investment you should fight for, it is the market that capitalizes on vanity or ‘self-investment’ stories that you shou rage against. Because every inch of love for the self you give away to the market is an inch you’ll have to battle to get back. Once its been commoditized and productized, that self-love ritual will never feel satisfying. You’ll miss the simple soap baths you took before the market turned them into self-love rituals.
In this world, the greatest act of love towards self is to become a warrior of your integrity and wholeness on a battlefield of fragmented views on your soul. Understand, with absolute clarity, that nobody and nothing has greater authority over your truth and over love of self that yourself. That is the greatest declaration of love. It’s a declaration of freedom. 
From this freedom you move from self-love as a programmable state of mind to the love of self as a state of truthful being.
The xx people who shared ‘you have to do the hard work yourself’ were those who escaped the rollercoaster of fabricated self-love highs and comedowns and started walking their path. Sometimes alone, sometimes wandering, and at other times grateful. How wonderful it is to walk your own path.
When you tap into the love of self, you’ll naturally behave like who you already are–a femme fatale or an outstanding inventor, a girl boss or the funniest person alive. Being who you are is your greatest life’s achievement. 
What is the love of self?
I recall a specific spring in Romania, during a time of economic fragility for my family. It was the season of Mărțișor, a spring celebration where w
e exchanged small tokens, like brooches tied with a pretty red and white string, called ‘martisoare’, to welcome the spring. In the schoolyard's hierarchy, the number of trinkets you received and wore on your chest was a direct metric of your social capital. By the same token, you’d have to gift one martisor back for each one received.
This spring, my family could not afford these martisoare. We could barely afford food. By the logic of the market, I was in a deficit. I was “less than.” Shame should have been the natural response. By the false wisdom of self-love narratives, I should have then loved myself first–either buying myself a martisor or repeating self worth affirmations to hype myself up for when I’d show up empty handed. I did none of this. 
Instead, I found scraps of red velvet left over from my mother’s sewing. I cut them into hearts, stuffed them with cotton, and sewed them with a white rim. I made thirty-five of them, for each of my classmates, my family, my friends, and teachers. Thirty five velvet heart-shaped martisoare.
On March 1st 1990, these velvet hearts became the most extraordinary thing that happened in school that day. Everyone got one. No heart was bigger than the other. They were made from pure love–unifying, healing, generative, overcoming poverty and shame. 
I did not give from a place of “lack”, I gave them from love, and self-love was absolutely part of it. I loved myself not because I had the “stuff,” but because I was the source of the stuff. 
Over the next decades of my life, I would re-enact this generating self-love experience with people. Despite any lack in my life, and regardless of how much I loved them, I would find myself in a state of unbeatable generosity and resourcefulness. I would generate gifts and moments of complete appreciation out of thin air. Somehow that generative power never consumed me, it was my power. 
Some of these people were men I loved, others were strangers, friends, colleagues. I know this type of self-love sounds familiar. Perhaps you’ve seen it in your mom, in your neighbour, in an outstanding woman who touched your heart. Perhaps you’ve seen this in yourself. As I look back on my pattern of generating more love out of self-love, as I write this sentence, I see the generative power of the love of self in everyone around me, and I feel overcome with awe. Look how precious our souls are. Look how powerful love is. 
The love of self is a state of being, as natural and fundamental as being alive. Humans experience it when they become aware of themselves as separate individuals, first in early childhood, although toddlers do not have the vocabulary to call it as such––they simply live it. Later in life, children are given words for their inner workings––like “I am good’, ‘I am beautiful’, ‘I can do things, I can play, I am confident’––to paint a picture of the many ways the love of self can be distinguished, labels, and fragmented in how we relate to the world.
The love of self is however detached from virtuous and moral labels. When you're at your lowest, when you have failed and made mistakes, you still find within yourself power to keep going and compassion for your failings. Some call it survival instinct, others resilience, self-sufficiency, or grace. Being unlovable and unloving is part of the love of self, too. In pop psychology and in psychoanalysis, this is called the ‘shadow’. You don’t need to reprogram it for a fee. The love of self is vast enough to hold your shame and your dark secrets. It doesn’t mean that loving yourself means tolerating the degradation of your life and potential that comes with indulging destructive and dark thoughts or habits. No. We don’t tolerate a decrepit garden, we tend it––some weeds are helpful to the entire ecosystem, others require pruning.
As we mature, we develop more skills to practically turn the love of self into adaptive responses, self-regulation, and ultimately self-mastery. The more psychologically mature we are, the more grounded our love of self. We’re less likely to buy into self-love programs or confuse it with self-gratification. 
And lastly, the love of self is generative. Earlier I said it’s a fundamental state of being, like being alive. I see the love of self to a life force in its own right: creative, evolving, and providing energy well beyond self-preservation. My 35 heart-shaped velvet martisoare were not an act of self-preservation, like trying to avoid feeling shame at school; they were evidence that being myself adds to life and to the world. 
How do you know you love yourself, and for what? That’s easy. First, you can’t storyboard who you really are, then pick and choose your best and least favorable traits. Workshops that facilitate storyboarding are helpful, but it’s easy to pick a few labels like ‘funny’, ‘friendly’ and make them your reasons for self-love. First, these reasons cannot depend on something else––in order to be funny, you need an audience. Maybe you’re not funny, but you feel that you’ve received appreciation for humour, so you stick with it until you truly understand who you are. Storyboarding can only get you as far as your tolerance limits.
The most truthful way to know the nature of your love of self is through projection. When you feel an affinity with someone, why is that? When you idealize and love someone–what image do you paint for them? Instead of recognizing your own wisdom, creativity, or strength, you attribute it to a partner or authority figure, putting them on a pedestal. That is positive projection, or as Robert A. Johnson calls it––seeing and giving away our gold.
My gold became clear with romantic partners. At 37 when I met a guy I fall in love with later. I didn’t plan on dating, I met him at live jazz gig. More or less reluctantly, I went on a first date. He was steady, calm, wholesome and unassuming, unlike other men who rushed to impress with little and seal the deal fast. The second date was on me. I bought him the best steak in London. At the time of that date I had just been made redundant and I was desperately searching for a job but I had my savings. And although I was battling with shame and uncertainty whilst losing my monthly income, I still decided to give this man the best and most expensive steak. I justified it by thinking that he is worthy of the best date––from what I had seen so far. The truth is, I was worthy of the best, and the qualities I saw in him were mine. It’s impossible to draw conclusions about one’s consistent character within weeks of casual chatting. I gave this man the best date I wish I would give would have given myself because I saw my own bright light in him. That’s how projection works: your mind sees and believes the best of another, but that ‘best’ is not theirs, it’s yours. If my best self were charming, extroverted, and easy-going, I’d have projected those qualities in others and loved them for my ‘best’. Projection is automatic and truthful, that’s why it is a more accurate path to understanding why you love yourself.
On the other end of projection, negative projection looks like contempt and hatred of another. Barring evidence that one’s behavior is malicious and repeatedly outside the bounds of human failings, dislike of another is a projection of what we do not like within and try hard to correct or cover. Contempt, however, cuts you away from your shadow. It implies a position of superiority and righteousness to be ‘cut off’ from anything disagreeable or what you lack empathy for. So, if we can’t have empathy for what could be part of our shadow, how could we possibly befriend it?
In our maturation journey, working with both positive and negative projection requires a much wider capacity for self-awareness. It requires that one can admit to oneself being capable of the same unacceptable behavior in certain circumstances. It’s much more than being humble and accepting. It’s love, tough love; because if you know you can be as lazy as that flatmate that constantly gets on your nerves, then you can address this laziness, rather than avoid admitting to it.
