On psychological maturity: stabilizing the love of self
Our brains are biologically intolerant of ambiguity. We are hard-wired for certainty—the psychological drive to organize random parts into a meaningful whole, even when that “whole” is a lie.
We see this in optical illusions, where our eyes automatically group objects to create shapes that don’t exist. We see it in our dreams, where noises from neighbors hanging pictures get instantly integrated into a dream narrative of someone banging on our door. We even see it in our social prejudices: we categorize a stranger by their clothes in milliseconds because the brain prefers false typologies over the discomfort of approaching and getting to know someone. Whether it is a prejudice, an optical illusion, or the reductive rhetoric of the “manosphere,” the mechanism is the same: the uncontrollable search and settling for immediate certainties. 
But there is a high price for this comfort. When the search for certainty replaces the foundation of the self, we settle for ready-made answers rather than deeper truths. We choose a fizzy drink that promises immediate energy over a healthy diet for a slower burn. If a ‘self-care workshop’ promises to boost your self-love, that looks like a better deal than making small healthy choices every day for years. To dig out the truth of anything that’s presented to us is hard, first because we have to decline immediate certainties thought out for us and temporary rewards. We have to ask more questions, do more work, sit longer with discomfort, and even fail a few times.
I saw the “ugly side” of this chase in the tech world. In my twenties, I lived for 48-hour startup hackathons. The premise was intoxicating: find a team, identify a problem, and build a Minimum Viable Product (MVP) in a weekend. As I moved from participant to judge, I watched hundreds of intelligent people fall into a “certainty trap.” Under time pressure, they abandoned deep market validation for great pitches that later fail due diligence. These eager entrepreneurs were building things fast, but they weren’t doing the right things because they couldn’t sit with the ambiguity of an assumption long enough to solve it. When asked for proof of traction, vanity metrics replaced truth: buying audiences replaced genuine interest, and high click-through rates replaced sustainable sales. Statistically, 90% of startups fail, and the majority of those failures stem from “building something nobody wants”—a direct result of choosing the false certainties over the ambiguity of a real human need.
I wonder how much of our lives we spend chasing false certainties that never pay off in lasting self-love. and how many conversations in the therapist’s room reflect the eventual realization that we still don’t know who we really are, what we truly want and what gives us purpose.
The foundation needed to know oneself and love oneself is built on truth, not on pressure nor false promise. How is psychological maturity connected to the love of self? 
The ‘self-love’ imagery that floods online forums is highly contagious, but it drives us further away from our self-love goals. A video of a confident, attractive woman with millions of likes fires up the social part of brain that makes emotions contagious. You want whatbecause of she has. As you continue watching, the emotion of self-love feels palpable––if only you did what she does or had what she had. It’s all a performance, but your mind cannot distinguish emotive content from your own desires and aspirations, it’s drawn in. This mechanism is the basis of automatic engagement that exploits our brain’s primal sensitivity to social cues and emotions to either garner engagement or influence behavior. It appeals to such deep layers of your primitive brain, that it’s very hard to escape it. Artificial intelligence algorithms take it further, learning to connect subtle reactions to the next-best-content, feeding us fabricated emotions and experiences, non-stop. 
Our sense of self is erased and replaced with feedback loops that induce positive or negative emotions. Instead of regulating ourselves, we are emotionally regulated by what we consume. Now rage-baited by ‘aging’ filters, next soothed by longevity remedies. Now riled up by #invisiblewomen trends, then propped up by the Dark Feminine trope. Biologically, the constant chemical flickering between “rage-bait” (cortisol) and “aesthetic-soothing” (dopamine) keeps the prefrontal cortex—the seat of rational decision-making—offline. You are literally in a state of functional decortication, stripping yourself of critical thinking, purpose, discernment and self-awareness. With your attention caught between chasing what one viral woman has and getting hooked on the next self-love content, your true identity is being eroded. The muscle of discernment weakens. Instead of standing up for what you really need, you stand mesmerized, doom-scrolling your day away. 
Emotional hijacking through machine learning is this century’s most damaging dark pattern of what should be an advanced technology for the benefit of humanity. It turns people into objects, easily tossed up or down your ‘for you page’. Most often, people are not aware of what’s happening, although they may admit of suffering from ‘brain rot’ and ‘doomscrolling’ when stressed. But it’s almost as if there’s not much anyone can do to resist the machine. A sense of powerlessness, and then it’s back to letting out emotions get used as engagement fuel. 
During doom-scrolling and online time we disappear to ourselves and we become someone else’s audience or click-through. The best I can describe this phenomenon is that we lose consciousness.
And without consciousness––without awareness of how one’s inner experience is shaped into a sense of self––we enter a state of decline in psychological maturity. Psychological maturity is a stage one reaches after having developed enough emotional and thinking skills to maintain agency and take accountability for one’s life––both potential and adversity. Whilst binge-watching videos feels too good to be described as ‘adversity’, it is the kind of benign harm we agree to overlook because it’s an easy comfort. But, it takes skills like the disciplined refusal to let your inner state be a “by-product” of a machine-learning feedback loop.
As for the love of self, although it’s yours since birth, it takes psychological maturity to protect and stabilize it.
Stabilizing the love of self requires what Robert A. Johnson calls “inner work”—the accountability, the self-reflection and the taking back the projections we have cast onto the digital screens. When you see the “perfect” woman online and feel a pang of lack, maturity allows you to pause and say:“That radiance I see in her is my unrealized potential.” Instead of buying the product she is selling, you do the hard work of developing the quality she is reflecting. 
This is the shift from the “programmable self” (which reacts to stimuli) to the “unprogrammable self” (which acts from essence). The machine thrives on your fragmentation—it wants you to be a collection of “likes,” “triggers,” and “purchases.” Psychological maturity is the act of becoming Integrated. As the amount of time spent online has increased by [XX% in the last decade], our “unconscious hours” have scaled at a terrifying rate. We have traded our Internal Locus of Control for an Algorithmic Locus of Control. To be mature in the 21st century is to be a “Warrior of Awareness.” It is the realization that your self-worth is not a “variable” to be optimized by a developer in Palo Alto, but a constant that you alone must protect.
Without this consciousness, we are merely “wetware”—biological hardware running someone else’s code. But with it, we become unprogrammable. We move from the “scam of self-love” to the authority of the Love of Self.